/* ============================================================================
midpoint-of-segment.epher -- midpoint of a segment

The midpoint of the segment from (x1, y1) to (x2, y2) is the average
of the endpoints, ((x1 + x2) / 2, (y1 + y2) / 2) (standard formula).
The results confirm the definition: a midpoint is equidistant from the
two ends, so the last result compares the two distances.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/geometry/coordinates/midpoint-of-segment.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\geometry\coordinates\midpoint-of-segment.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/geometry/coordinates/midpoint-of-segment.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

The endpoints to try are in the results section below; edit them
there.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the model ----------------------------------------------------------------
def mid(p, q) = {(p[1] + q[1]) / 2, (p[2] + q[2]) / 2}
def dist(p, q) = sqrt((p[1] - q[1]) ^ 2 + (p[2] - q[2]) ^ 2)   // for the check
def same(p, q) = dist(p, mid(p, q)) == dist(q, mid(p, q))

// ---- results ------------------------------------------------------------------
print("midpoint of (1, 3)-(5, 7):", mid({1, 3}, {5, 7}))
print("midpoint of (0, 0)-(2, 4):", mid({0, 0}, {2, 4}))
print("equidistant from both ends:", same({1, 3}, {5, 7}))
/* ---- expected output ----
= midpoint of (1, 3)-(5, 7): {3, 5}
= midpoint of (0, 0)-(2, 4): {1, 2}
= equidistant from both ends: true
*/
